"<div><div class=\" response-message\"></div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h1>Poll Insights</h1>\n<p><em>Generating insights...</em></p>\n</div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><p>This analysis is based on a small-scale pilot survey ($N=3$) targeting a specific demographic: female humanities students aged 18–24.</p>\n<h3>1. Key Findings and Trends</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The &quot;Cautious Middle&quot; Sentiment:</strong> The mean score for recommendation (Q1) is exactly <strong>3.00</strong>, with a spread from 2 to 4. This indicates a lack of &quot;promoters&quot; (scores of 9–10 in NPS terms) and a high concentration of &quot;passives&quot; or &quot;detractors.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Accuracy as a Baseline:</strong> 100% of respondents selected &quot;Accuracy&quot; as the most valuable feature. This suggests that for this cohort, accuracy is a &quot;hygiene factor&quot;—a requirement for entry—rather than a competitive advantage that drives high recommendation scores.</li>\n<li><strong>The &quot;Research Assistant&quot; Paradox:</strong> Qualitative data reveals a consistent trend: respondents value AI for <strong>organizational tasks</strong> (brainstorming, research assistance) but deeply distrust it for <strong>analytical or creative tasks</strong>. They view AI as a tool for the &quot;process&quot; but a threat to the &quot;product.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Demographic Patterns and Differences</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Extreme Homogeneity:</strong> All three respondents share identical demographic profiles (18–24, Female, Suburban, Some College). This eliminates demographic variance, making this less of a &quot;survey&quot; and more of a <strong>persona deep-dive</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychographic Alignment:</strong> Despite the small sample, there is a strong shared identity as &quot;Humanities Students.&quot; All three responses mention &quot;academic integrity,&quot; &quot;critical thinking,&quot; and the &quot;human soul/element.&quot; This suggests that for this specific group, the resistance to AI is philosophical and tied to their professional identity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Response Consistency and Quality</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High Engagement:</strong> The average response length is <strong>849 characters</strong>, which is exceptionally high for a digital poll. This indicates that the topic is highly polarizing or personally significant to the respondents.</li>\n<li><strong>Internal Validity:</strong> There is high consistency between the numerical ratings in Q1 and the sentiment in Q3.\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>&quot;2&quot; rating</strong> respondent uses words like &quot;terrifying&quot; and &quot;skeptical.&quot;</li>\n<li>The <strong>&quot;4&quot; rating</strong> respondent uses words like &quot;genuinely enthusiastic&quot; and &quot;free research assistant.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Potential Synthetic Pattern:</strong> The identical demographics and the highly structured, similarly phrased long-form answers (all mentioning &quot;humanities student,&quot; &quot;academic integrity,&quot; and &quot;critical thinking&quot;) are characteristic of <strong>synthetic data (AI-generated responses)</strong> or a very specific prompt-based simulation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4. Potential Biases or Limitations</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sample Size ($N=3$):</strong> The results are not statistically significant and cannot be generalized to the broader population or even the broader student body.</li>\n<li><strong>Selection Bias:</strong> The poll only captures the &quot;Humanities&quot; perspective. STEM students might prioritize &quot;Accuracy&quot; for different reasons and likely would not share the same &quot;existential&quot; concerns about AI replacing their &quot;original voice.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Lack of Feature Variance:</strong> Since 100% chose &quot;Accuracy,&quot; we have no data on how they value Speed, Ease of Use, or Cost, even though &quot;student budget&quot; was mentioned in the text.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>5. Recommendations for Survey Improvement</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Expand Q2 Options:</strong> Since &quot;Accuracy&quot; was a unanimous choice, add a &quot;Ranking&quot; question or a &quot;Constant Sum&quot; question (e.g., &quot;Distribute 100 points across these 5 features&quot;) to see the relative importance of accuracy vs. other factors.</li>\n<li><strong>Introduce Demographic Diversity:</strong> To gain actionable insights, the next iteration must include different majors (STEM vs. Arts), age groups (Graduate students vs. Undergrads), and genders.</li>\n<li><strong>Quantify the &quot;Integrity&quot; Concern:</strong> Add a Likert scale question specifically about &quot;Anxiety regarding Academic Integrity&quot; to quantify the qualitative concerns raised in Q3.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>6. Implications for Real-World Polling</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The &quot;Trust Gap&quot; in Education:</strong> For AI companies, these results suggest that &quot;Accuracy&quot; is not enough to win over the education sector. There is a significant emotional and ethical barrier regarding &quot;Academic Integrity&quot; that must be addressed through product features (e.g., citation transparency, &quot;human-in-the-loop&quot; workflows).</li>\n<li><strong>Value of Qualitative Data:</strong> This poll demonstrates that while the quantitative data (Mean: 3.0) is mediocre, the qualitative data is &quot;gold.&quot; It explains the <em>why</em> behind the stagnation in user adoption among certain groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Persona-Based Marketing:</strong> The data suggests that marketing AI to humanities students as a &quot;writer&quot; is a mistake; it should be marketed as a &quot;research librarian&quot; or &quot;organizational assistant&quot; to align with their comfort levels.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></div><div><div class=\"spinner-border\" role=\"status\"><span class=\"sr-only\">Loading...</span></div></div>"